  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Pleasant and dry for most through Labor Day\n\nIn the wake of Saturday's cold front, a refreshingly low\ndewpoint airmass has settled in across Pennsylvania and will\nensure pleasant conditions through Labor Day. An upper low\nswirling around in New England will keep upper air temperatures\ncool enough in PA to support widespread stratocumulus\ndevelopment this afternoon along with a few widely scattered\nshowers. But an otherwise nice day is in store with seasonable\nhigh temperatures in the 70s.\n\nHigh pressure will build overhead tonight, with calm winds,\nclear skies, and antecedent moisture supporting widespread\nvalley fog development by Monday morning. Lows will range from\nthe mid 40s in the northwest mountains to mid 50s in the\nsoutheast. No doubt a perfect night to open the windows!\n\nAfter morning fog mixes out, Labor Day will be about as good as\nit gets. A few wispy cirrus clouds will accentuate bright blue\nskies with abundant sunshine, passing fair weather cumulus\nclouds, and seasonable temperatures. The only minor complaint\ncould be little to no wind, but shaded areas will be absolutely\nmint.\n\n   ----------------------------------------------------\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: Uptick in heat and humidity through midweek\n\nAs the center of high pressure drifts southeast of PA on Tuesday\nand Wednesday, return southwesterly flow will usher in an uptick\nand heat and humidity. The never ending ridge of high pressure\ncentered over the Central US will also begin to nudge into the\nMid Atlantic as upper troughing over the Northeast retreats and\nthe amplified upper level pattern begins to flatten out a bit. Highs\non Tuesday will range from the mid 70s to mid 80s and Wednesday\nwill trend about 5 degrees warmer areawide. Combined with \ndewpoints back in the mid to upper 60s, heat index values may \ncrest the 90F mark in the south central mountains. Dry \nconditions will prevail and we'll continue to enjoy a break from\nvery active stretch of severe weather we've dealt with this \nsummer.\n\n   ----------------------------------------------------\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3: Showers, storms, and gusty winds Wednesday night & Thursday\n\nBy Wednesday night, a rapidly low pressure system will be moving\nthrough northern Ontario with an attached cold front extending \nsouth through the Great Lakes and Ohio Valley region. The \npressure gradient between a 1024mb high pressure system over the\nAtlantic and a sub 990mb low in Canada will generate a notable\nuptick in synoptic scale winds Wednesday night into Thursday.\nIt's been awhile since we've had widespread gusty winds, but\nwe're getting into the time of year where such a weather pattern\nbecomes more common. \n\nGusty winds will ramp up Wednesday during the day out of the\nsouthwest ahead of an approaching cold front, and continue\nthrough Thursday afternoon shifting to a westerly direction \nafter the front passes. Wind gusts of 25 to 35 mph seem \nrealistic at this point, with the highest readings occurring at\nhigher elevations in the Laurel Highlands.\n\nThe cold front will also bring a chance for showers and storms\nahead of and along the front. The risk for severe weather will\ndepend on the magnitude of a shortwave trough racing through \nthe region and any associated jet dynamics in play. AI NWP \nguidance highlights the potential for severe weather in \nsoutheast PA at this time, but percentages remain low (the \nequivalent of a Marginal, level 1 out of 5, Risk) due to \nlingering uncertainty. Vertical wind profiles look \nunidirectional, so the primary hazard would be from damaging \nwinds.\n\nHigh pressure builds behind the cold front and the end of the\nweek/start of the weekend looks sunny and dry with low humidity.",
